rove their standard of  living there's no question that if our  elected officials at least at the  national level learned about mmt  understood it and and maybe adopted it  that we would have a very very different  way of governing do you see any evidence  that that is starting to happen that it  could happen I think that they they  operate on the principles of mmt again  the current tax discussions revealed  that the Republicans never took  seriously the debt and the deficit  whenever they want their policy  priorities the debt and deficit is not  just secondary it's it's it's not at all  at the point of discussion but Democrats  have to change because they continue to  harp on this debt deficit problem so we  number one the tax discussion showed  that this is a very  critical attitude that the debt and  deficit really don't matter in terms of  their size what matters is what we do  with the expenditure and what I would  like to see is more people begin to  think this way because then we will be  asking the questions have we really  produced full employment do people have  decent jobs decent incomes do we have  the investment that we need for our  economy do we have inflation  you know mmt will be concerned about  inflation at full employment or even  before there these are the real  questions not the size of the debt and  the deficit so I believe that the  current political environment operates  certainly on the Republican side  operates on the principles of mmt but  the rhetoric is used to undermine  privatize other important programs we've  been speaking about modern monetary  theory we're going to continue covering  it and we've been speaking with Pavlina  Chernova who's associate professor of  economics and director of the economics  program at Bard College thanks so much  for joining us again my pleasure

