it to is that entire  conversation a red herring in other  words does the debt matter it depends on  what you know what you're trying to  accomplish the debt and the deficit are  just simply accounting results of what  we have done yes they matter in terms of  how they were incurred so if we had a  great deficit as a consequence of giving  enormous tax cuts to the top and that  did very little for the economy yes it  matters it no but if we incurred  expenditure that produced employment  investment etc then yes it matters as  well it's just you have to look at the  real reserve results what doesn't matter  is its size in and of itself so a lot of  people are believed there's a magical  number of the deficit or the debt beyond  which some terrible things will happen  to the economy in this sense modern  money says that this is totally the  wrong thing to be looking at the first  thing that we like to stress and this is  kind of an important point  the phobia of deficit spending needs to  go away  the deficit is an absolute necessity for  the economy the reason is that the  deficit is simply the difference between  what government spends and what it taxes  back and if the government said spent a  hundred billion dollars but only taxes  eighty back the 20 billion dollars  remain in the economy that is savings  those are net savings in the private  sector so this is a simple accounting  identity this is a simple simple math if  government spends more than it takes  back the different stays in our pockets  and that's called our savings we like to  save we like to put away money for  retirement for college education etc so  because we the private sector like to  save the deficit has to exist the  question is where did the money go  whose pockets did it fill did it go  again all the way to the top where it  was maybe invested in the stock market  or did it go in the pockets of firms  intrapreneurs households that are trying  to meet their daily demands and  essentially improve their standard of  living there's no question that if our  elected officials at least at the  national level learned about mmt  understood it and and maybe adopted it  that we would have a very very different  way of governing do you see any evidence  that that is starting to happen that it  could happen I think that they they  operate on the principles of mmt again  the current tax discussions revealed  that the Republicans never took  seriously the debt and the deficit  whenever they want their policy  priorities the debt and deficit is not  just secondary it's it's it's not at all  at the point of discussion but Democrats  have to change because they continue to  harp on this debt deficit problem so we  number one the tax discussion showed  that this is a very  critical attitude that the debt and  deficit really don't matter in terms of  their size what matters is what we do  with the expenditure and what I would  like to see is more people begin to  think this way because then we will be  asking the questions have we really  produced full employment do people have  decent jobs decent incomes do we have  the investment that we need for our  economy do we have inflation  you know mmt will be concerned about  inflation at full employment or even  before there these are the real  questions not the size of the debt and  the deficit so I believe that the  current political environment operates  certainly on the Republican side  operates on the principles of mmt but  the rhetoric is used to undermine  privatize other important programs we've  been speaking about modern monetary  theory we're going to continue covering  it and we've been speaking with Pavlina  Chernova who's associate professor of  economics and director of the economics  program at Bard College thanks so much  for joining us again my pleasure
 

